WEBVTT

00:02.377 --> 00:05.422
You're doing quite well, aren't you?

00:05.505 --> 00:07.590
As Xeno likes to say,

00:07.674 --> 00:10.218
"It's so…

00:12.345 --> 00:13.805
elegant!

00:16.891 --> 00:18.018
Luna.

00:19.728 --> 00:22.981
Did Dr. Xeno use to be a NASA scientist?

00:23.690 --> 00:25.150
How would you know that?

00:31.573 --> 00:36.202
<i>Dr. Xeno was,</i>
<i>from the very beginning, my mentor…</i>

00:41.458 --> 00:44.335
…in the science of rocket-making.

00:47.046 --> 00:51.217
ORIGINAL WORK: RIICHIRO INAGAKI, BOICHI
SHUEISHA JUMP COMICS

02:33.862 --> 02:36.406
Good. Let's try it.

02:52.630 --> 02:54.048
What is the cause?

02:56.134 --> 02:58.803
No, I don't understand a thing at all!

03:03.725 --> 03:06.686
We can look up all the secrets
of rocket science on the computer.

03:08.396 --> 03:11.733
You understand English?
That's impressive, Senku!

03:11.816 --> 03:15.737
Not really.
I haven't gone beyond "This is a pen."

03:15.820 --> 03:17.572
"This is…" what did you say?

03:17.655 --> 03:20.700
However, most papers
are usually in English.

03:20.783 --> 03:23.161
I have to read them
with a dictionary in hand.

03:23.912 --> 03:26.873
But there are limits
to just reading papers.

03:26.956 --> 03:29.959
It's quicker to share
information with colleagues.

03:30.043 --> 03:31.878
That's what science is all about.

03:37.800 --> 03:40.553
Are you sending this out
to the whole world?

03:40.637 --> 03:43.848
Yep, it's basically spam.

03:52.565 --> 03:54.442
A grade schooler emailed us a question.

03:55.026 --> 03:58.112
"If outer space is black,
why is the sky blue?"

03:58.196 --> 04:01.366
How cute! Be sure to answer them.

04:01.449 --> 04:03.243
This one's from a Japanese student.

04:03.826 --> 04:05.745
I see, his name is Senku.

04:06.913 --> 04:09.958
He did his best
to write the question in English.

04:10.041 --> 04:11.376
What is it?

04:11.459 --> 04:12.919
TITLE:
FROM: SENKU

04:17.131 --> 04:20.718
<i>My rocket research started</i>
<i>by heavily modifying a car turbocharger.</i>

04:20.802 --> 04:24.305
<i>However, relying on papers kept leading me</i>
<i>to obstacles to a ridiculous extent.</i>

04:24.389 --> 04:27.517
<i>The first issue I encountered was</i>
<i>the oscillation in combustion pressure.</i>

04:27.600 --> 04:29.978
<i>I wanted to use computer simulations</i>
<i>to keep the error</i>

04:30.061 --> 04:33.940
<i>of my "mid-frequency</i>
<i>oscillating combustion"</i>

04:34.023 --> 04:36.484
<i>within seven percent</i>
<i>by adjusting the injector’s structure.</i>

04:36.567 --> 04:39.570
<i>But with my weak PC, this woudn't be</i>
<i>possible even in ten billion years.</i>

04:39.654 --> 04:42.282
<i>I installed a VPN on the research server</i>

04:42.365 --> 04:43.992
<i>at the university where my dad works…</i>

04:44.075 --> 04:46.661
Isn't he confessing a crime?

04:47.245 --> 04:49.539
There's no way this is a grade schooler.

04:49.622 --> 04:51.291
An adult must be pulling this prank.

04:51.374 --> 04:53.209
Anyway, it's what it is.

04:53.293 --> 04:55.920
Let's stay out of it and just ignore it.

05:10.810 --> 05:12.729
You did it, Senku!

05:12.812 --> 05:16.691
You received an answer
from a professional working at NASA!

05:16.774 --> 05:18.109
You're too loud.

05:19.527 --> 05:21.779
<i>I've written down a few formulas,</i>

05:21.863 --> 05:25.366
<i>but in conclusion,</i>
<i>you'll need a niobium alloy nozzle.</i>

05:26.075 --> 05:27.702
<i>Dr. Senku,</i>

05:27.785 --> 05:31.372
<i>it is probably the data preview</i>
<i>that got you lost.</i>

05:32.081 --> 05:34.625
<i>Your measuring instruments</i>
<i>might be of low quality.</i>

05:34.709 --> 05:38.546
<i>You should get a platinum-rhodium</i>
<i>alloy temperature sensor</i>

05:38.629 --> 05:40.131
<i>with a low-resistance thermocouple.</i>

05:41.132 --> 05:43.051
<i>Regards, NASA's Dr. X.</i>

05:46.095 --> 05:48.514
Here you go, Senku.

05:48.598 --> 05:49.766
Thank you.

05:51.184 --> 05:54.103
It'd be great if they got you
the thermometer in the picture.

05:54.187 --> 05:55.396
Do they sell them at 100 yen?

05:56.022 --> 05:57.732
I wonder how much these are.

06:01.319 --> 06:02.945
PLATINUM…

06:03.529 --> 06:04.864
PLATINUM-RHODIUM ALLOY TEMPERATURE SENSOR

06:14.165 --> 06:16.000
Senku!

06:16.584 --> 06:19.879
I can't believe you came
all the way to America!

06:19.962 --> 06:23.049
-I'm feeling homesick…
-Well, can I borrow that thing?

06:24.175 --> 06:25.760
The magic card.

06:25.843 --> 06:27.470
Sure, here it is.

06:28.346 --> 06:31.974
The devilish credit card
that lets you get for free

06:32.058 --> 06:35.311
anything science-related
that astronauts receive

06:35.394 --> 06:37.355
when they begin training in America!

06:37.438 --> 06:39.941
How did you know that?

06:40.024 --> 06:43.277
I mean you're right, but an online
search wouldn't tell you that much.

06:44.821 --> 06:46.572
You're going back to Japan already?

06:46.656 --> 06:50.785
Did you fail the in-flight
American Ultra Quiz?

06:50.868 --> 06:54.288
That's a generational thing, old man.
I don’t get the reference.

06:54.372 --> 06:56.165
When are you coming again?

06:56.249 --> 06:58.459
When it's time to renew your credit card.

06:58.543 --> 07:01.295
How could you be so cold to your father?

07:01.379 --> 07:03.798
PLATINUM-RHODIUM ALLOY
TEMPERATURE SENSOR ¥500,000

07:03.881 --> 07:05.925
XENON GAS ¥600,000

07:06.008 --> 07:08.052
OPTICAL PHASE CONTRAST MICROSCOPE
¥1,000,000

07:08.136 --> 07:10.680
THERMAL CAMERA ¥1,500,000

07:12.098 --> 07:15.726
These look expensive!
I have no idea what any of them are.

07:15.810 --> 07:18.855
You know,
I still could make do without them.

07:19.439 --> 07:22.900
But I'll gratefully take as much as I can

07:22.984 --> 07:24.485
from this heap of equipment.

07:25.236 --> 07:29.449
<i>With Dr. X's online course,</i>

07:31.409 --> 07:34.745
<i>I'm going to research it all</i>
<i>and try it all.</i>

07:36.164 --> 07:40.793
<i>We're not following in the footsteps</i>
<i>of two million years of human history,</i>

07:41.627 --> 07:43.754
<i>but rather,</i>
<i>we're standing on its shoulders!</i>

08:52.865 --> 08:57.453
But Senku,
aren't you aiming to make a rocket?

08:57.537 --> 09:01.290
What if you just learn
the relevant science for that?

09:03.376 --> 09:07.171
Where's the challenge in knowing
what science would be relevant?

09:07.922 --> 09:11.926
That's like saying I can just
buy the winning ticket to get rich.

09:12.009 --> 09:15.972
There's always more we've yet known.
That's what science's all about.

09:16.055 --> 09:16.973
NEW MESSAGE

09:20.101 --> 09:23.145
<i>Science is power.</i>

09:25.523 --> 09:29.443
<i>For example, it's needless to say</i>
<i>that your rocket</i>

09:29.527 --> 09:33.114
<i>is essentially the same thing</i>
<i>as a ballistic missile, right?</i>

09:33.823 --> 09:35.324
<i>With science,</i>

09:35.408 --> 09:40.913
<i>the wise can guide and lead the masses</i>
<i>in the right direction.</i>

09:41.914 --> 09:46.377
<i>It's an elegant form of power, Dr. Senku.</i>

09:46.460 --> 09:50.756
I have no interest in that.

09:50.840 --> 09:54.343
I just want to understand
the secrets of the world and outer space

09:54.427 --> 09:57.013
to a ridiculous extent.

09:58.347 --> 10:01.100
I want to know all of them.

10:03.102 --> 10:05.479
I'm ten billion percent sure!

10:30.671 --> 10:34.759
Xeno, what would you do if you transported
yourself to the Stone Age?

10:39.096 --> 10:41.599
I would create
scientific weapons from scratch

10:41.682 --> 10:43.934
and become a dictator.

10:44.977 --> 10:47.438
You, a dictator?

10:49.565 --> 10:52.401
What if more scientists joined you?

10:52.485 --> 10:53.569
Yes!

10:54.278 --> 10:57.281
No matter the situation,
humanity will always rise.

10:57.365 --> 11:01.410
I'm ten billion percent sure
that someday, somewhere,

11:01.494 --> 11:05.706
someone who craves all knowledge
of how the world works will emerge.

11:06.666 --> 11:09.210
Someone like my son.

11:11.671 --> 11:16.217
In that case, it'd be a competition
among those who emerge.

11:16.300 --> 11:20.638
I wonder who would conquer science
in a brand new Stone Age.

11:22.973 --> 11:27.186
<i>In this way,</i>
<i>the temperature inside the rocket,</i>

11:27.269 --> 11:30.356
<i>or rather, the homemade missile,</i>
<i>can be kept at 60ºC,</i>

11:30.439 --> 11:35.361
<i>meaning it can be loaded with biological</i>
<i>weapons like viruses and bacteria.</i>

11:36.153 --> 11:40.866
I guess I should at least know about it.

11:43.452 --> 11:47.289
<i>So, sulfa drugs would work</i>
<i>with pneumococcus too, right?</i>

11:48.916 --> 11:51.043
Where are you going, Senku?

11:51.127 --> 11:53.129
-Africa.
-What?

12:06.100 --> 12:07.685
My son is coming to visit!

12:08.769 --> 12:10.438
You two get on well, don't you?

12:10.521 --> 12:15.025
<i>Actually, he's only coming</i>
<i>because my credit card has expired.</i>

12:15.860 --> 12:19.447
He's waiting outside. His name's Senku.

12:21.115 --> 12:23.033
Senku?

13:03.073 --> 13:04.825
Hey!

13:04.909 --> 13:07.828
Senku!

13:09.413 --> 13:10.915
What is it?

13:12.166 --> 13:13.167
Well…

13:52.164 --> 13:53.582
ARE SWALLOWS TURNING INTO STONE!?

14:02.758 --> 14:05.177
The amount of social media posts by region

14:05.261 --> 14:08.847
is closely related to the real-world
distribution of swallows.

14:08.931 --> 14:11.850
There's no doubt
that real swallows have turned into stone.

14:12.434 --> 14:14.270
That's an interesting perspective.

14:14.353 --> 14:18.524
It couldn't have occurred to me,
an old man who doesn't like smartphones.

14:18.607 --> 14:20.734
That's science for the younger generation.

14:20.818 --> 14:22.653
It wasn't me.

14:22.736 --> 14:26.031
The one who uploaded that data analysis
was a high school student…

14:28.868 --> 14:30.286
<i>from Japan.</i>

14:38.544 --> 14:41.714
US CENTER FOR DISEASE
CONTROL AND PREVENTION

14:43.090 --> 14:45.509
It even has the internal organs.

14:45.593 --> 14:47.219
There's no way it's a sculpture.

14:47.803 --> 14:50.014
It's extremely water-repellent.

14:50.097 --> 14:54.226
Its toughness varies greatly
from the center to the periphery.

14:54.310 --> 14:56.353
Even the smallest parts
are extremely hard.

14:56.937 --> 14:59.064
What are the results
of the X-ray analysis?

14:59.148 --> 15:03.611
It's mainly iron, with traces of gold.
Also carbon and nitrogen.

15:03.694 --> 15:06.655
It's unresponsive to every medication.

15:06.739 --> 15:09.116
Only nitric acid causes
a slight ion leakage.

15:09.742 --> 15:10.868
What about UV rays?

15:10.951 --> 15:13.662
No embrittlement short-wave EM
has been detected.

15:13.746 --> 15:15.122
This is absurd…

15:15.205 --> 15:19.752
Response by wavelength unknown,
structure is unclear.

15:19.835 --> 15:22.087
It's the first time
I've seen this mineral.

15:22.171 --> 15:25.132
I'm not even sure
if I should call it a mineral.

15:26.592 --> 15:30.763
<i>This is an abnormal phenomenon</i>
<i>on a global and cosmic scale.</i>

15:31.388 --> 15:33.265
<i>Dr. Xeno from NASA,</i>

15:33.349 --> 15:35.267
<i>we need your knowledge on this.</i>

15:39.021 --> 15:43.192
<i>It sinks in water,</i>
<i>but it's still light for a stone.</i>

15:43.859 --> 15:47.905
<i>It looks like pumice.</i>
<i>Is it a porous stone?</i>

15:49.949 --> 15:52.159
<i>Let's try this.</i>

15:52.242 --> 15:53.285
HIROSUE HIGH SCHOOL

15:53.369 --> 15:55.871
I'll make gasoline out of a bottle cap.

15:56.705 --> 15:59.124
I would normally use porous zeolite,

15:59.708 --> 16:03.295
but I wonder if the petrified swallow's
wings would work too.

16:03.921 --> 16:05.547
Listen up, Senku!

16:05.631 --> 16:08.550
I've made a decision.
Right now, at this very moment

16:08.634 --> 16:11.887
I'll confess to Yuzuriha
my feelings for the last five years.

16:13.055 --> 16:16.475
Wow, that sounds really interesting.

16:17.559 --> 16:20.229
All right, I made some gasoline.

16:20.896 --> 16:23.440
If we put it in a nuclear
magnetic resonance spectrometer,

16:23.524 --> 16:25.234
the distribution of carbon chain lengths

16:25.317 --> 16:29.321
may tell us something about the the depth
of the pores and its physical properties.

16:30.823 --> 16:31.907
I'll research it all.

16:35.911 --> 16:37.454
<i>I'll try it all.</i>

16:44.461 --> 16:47.339
DARPA, the Defense Advanced
Research Projects Agency

16:47.423 --> 16:49.591
is having a tech fest today…

16:50.300 --> 16:51.885
supposedly.

16:52.511 --> 16:56.056
Were the Special Forces even called in?

16:56.140 --> 16:59.601
It reeks of so much trouble
that it’s messing me up inside.

17:01.562 --> 17:02.646
Isn't this a gathering

17:03.230 --> 17:07.151
of America's top superheroes?
What are we celebrating?

17:08.360 --> 17:09.403
This.

17:09.987 --> 17:11.822
Maybe a terrorist attack
with an unknown weapon,

17:11.905 --> 17:13.532
or an assault by another country.

17:14.408 --> 17:15.284
Dr. Xeno!

17:15.367 --> 17:18.704
Now's not the time to reveal that yet.

17:18.787 --> 17:21.457
It doesn't matter, anyway.

17:21.540 --> 17:25.544
It will be revealed to the public
after tomorrow's experts meeting.

17:26.962 --> 17:30.049
Placing the glass wool
soaked in nitric acid on the terminal

17:30.132 --> 17:31.675
and applying it to the potentiometer…

17:31.759 --> 17:35.554
Okay Xeno, that's enough.
Get to the point.

17:41.351 --> 17:43.395
What is this?

17:43.479 --> 17:46.690
A very slight potential difference
appears only around the head.

17:47.983 --> 17:49.193
They are brain waves.

17:50.194 --> 17:53.113
Actually, isn't it elegant?

17:53.197 --> 17:55.574
This stone statue is still alive!

17:55.657 --> 17:56.909
But as time passes,

17:56.992 --> 18:00.579
their brain waves will fade one by one,
as if they were falling asleep.

18:00.662 --> 18:04.666
But these swallows are still awake!

18:04.750 --> 18:08.796
You mean, if those struck with this
new weapon stay awake and determined,

18:08.879 --> 18:11.298
they have a chance to survive.

18:14.927 --> 18:15.844
My lady!

18:15.928 --> 18:19.848
I've prepared something I thought
would be just right for you.

18:21.058 --> 18:22.851
Please, accept this ring!

18:23.936 --> 18:27.731
Don't be so full of yourself,
Carlos, you stalker!

18:28.857 --> 18:29.733
My lady?

18:31.360 --> 18:32.486
What…

18:34.738 --> 18:36.115
What's that light?

18:43.497 --> 18:44.498
<i>Is this…</i>

18:45.207 --> 18:47.167
Evacuate! Take cover!

18:48.001 --> 18:50.671
Petrification is probably inevitable!

18:50.754 --> 18:53.674
The real challenge comes after!
Don't lose consciousness!

18:53.757 --> 18:57.427
We've got to protect my lady
with our lives, Carlos!

18:57.511 --> 18:58.679
Of course!

18:58.762 --> 19:01.431
<i>But damn, the ring!</i>

19:01.515 --> 19:05.435
<i>This is for my lady,</i>
<i>I don't want to lose it!</i>

19:05.519 --> 19:08.147
<i>I'm sorry, I'll wash it later!</i>

19:26.790 --> 19:27.708
<i>Damn.</i>

19:27.791 --> 19:31.503
<i>These are Captain Stanley's orders.</i>
<i>Don't lose consciousness…</i>

19:32.296 --> 19:35.674
<i>It's useless… I can't move an inch…</i>

19:38.594 --> 19:40.888
<i>No way!</i>

19:40.971 --> 19:44.308
<i>I'm an all-mighty woman</i>
<i>who was going to date an all-mighty man!</i>

19:44.391 --> 19:47.060
<i>We were going to be the coolest couple!</i>

19:47.144 --> 19:51.356
<i>I don't want to die like this!</i>

19:51.440 --> 19:54.151
<i>The soldier was</i>
<i>yelling at us to stay awake!</i>

19:54.234 --> 19:56.570
<i>I'm definitely not going to fall asleep!</i>

19:56.653 --> 20:00.490
<i>I'm Luna, the cool woman!</i>
<i>I'm Luna, the all-mighty woman!</i>

20:00.574 --> 20:03.911
<i>I'm Luna, the cool woman!</i>
<i>I'm Luna, the all-mighty woman!</i>

20:03.994 --> 20:08.540
<i>I won't let my lady get hurt.</i>
<i>I'll protect her with my life!</i>

20:10.083 --> 20:15.380
<i>I'm used to not being able to move</i>
<i>without falling asleep for hours.</i>

20:15.464 --> 20:18.217
<i>I'm a sniper, after all…</i>

20:43.575 --> 20:46.578
<i>The Pinnacles National Park we were in</i>

20:46.662 --> 20:48.497
<i>is full of saltpeter caves</i>

20:49.623 --> 20:51.250
<i>as well as nitric acid.</i>

20:52.209 --> 20:54.753
<i>I don't know</i>
<i>if that's for the better or the worse,</i>

20:54.836 --> 20:57.297
<i>but there's a possibility</i>
<i>that a change could happen.</i>

21:06.598 --> 21:11.728
<i>One, two, three, four, five,</i>

21:11.812 --> 21:15.107
<i>six, seven, eight, nine…</i>

21:15.816 --> 21:20.570
<i>9,157,661,240 seconds.</i>

21:21.196 --> 21:25.325
<i>As long as I stay conscious,</i>
<i>there's a chance for me to wake up.</i>

21:27.369 --> 21:29.913
<i>If I could wake up,</i>

21:29.997 --> 21:34.960
<i>I should aim to develop yellow dent corn,</i>
<i>which can be used both as livestock feed</i>

21:35.043 --> 21:36.628
<i>and to produce alcohol for fuel.</i>

21:37.671 --> 21:40.215
<i>The corn belt on the West Coast</i>
<i>is the right place.</i>

21:40.299 --> 21:41.842
<i>If I produced a hybrid type…</i>

21:41.925 --> 21:47.139
<i>No… Hopefully, the whole seed</i>
<i>will remain dormant in the strata.</i>

21:48.432 --> 21:52.644
<i>If there were any other scientists,</i>
<i>we would reunite there.</i>

21:56.523 --> 21:59.651
<i>This isn't a matter of fate.</i>

21:59.735 --> 22:02.571
<i>It's not a coincidence, either.</i>
<i>It's how it is…</i>

22:03.697 --> 22:07.326
<i>Our continuity is in the hands…</i>

22:13.165 --> 22:14.416
<i>of science!</i>

23:53.348 --> 23:55.934
NEXT EPISODE

23:56.017 --> 23:57.894
THIS WORK OF FICTION FEATURES ORGANISMS,
MATERIAL AND PROCESSES BASED ON REAL LIFE.

23:57.978 --> 23:59.688
COLLECTING OR REPLICATING THEM
MAY BE DANGEROUS AND ILLEGAL,

23:59.771 --> 24:00.939
SO DO NOT ATTEMPT.

24:01.022 --> 24:01.898
Subtitle translation by: Silvia
